-- a place (part of language-implementation-remainder; Avik, 2026-11-22: "Why would I have a single
-- centralized monolith"): a URL that stores bytes under a name and gives them back, and computes
-- nothing (HYPERACTIVE_PLACE: a bucket, or a directory). `hyperactive send` packs the objects the
-- pool gained since the last send (tar, by the time of the last send), stores the pack under its
-- hash (packs/HASH) and stores under the root's hash the packs that hold what it names
-- (roots/ROOT); `hyperactive take ROOT` fetches the packs of ROOT this machine has not had, each
-- kept only when its hash is its name and only its objects of the pool taken out, then takes the
-- root. Every command runs here; the place runs none
hy-sha256 : List Word → Proc W (Word × List Word)
hy-sha256 file = h-capture (cons "/bin/sh" (cons "-c" (cons "IFS=; sha256sum $1 2>/dev/null || shasum -a 256 $1" (cons "_" (cons file nil)))))
